# Theme 1 · reading 1 (Bakker & Demerouti, 2024) · step 3 "Key concepts": new teaching cards (TEACHV2 format)
import re,sys
LBL="What are job demands and job resources?|Why is JD-R called a flexible theory?|What do job demands do to you over time?|What do job resources do for you?|Do demands and resources work separately?|Where do personal resources come in?|Can employees change their own job?|What happens when strain takes over?|What can you now answer?"
C=[
'''<p class="zero qstart"><span class="qq">What does job demands-resources (JD-R) theory actually claim about work, well-being and performance?</span><b>Why this matters.</b> Step 2 showed why the article exists: the theory looks simple, but people use it wrongly. Before you can follow the 13 questions the authors answer, you need the theory itself. The authors give it in seven propositions, seven rules (pp. 188–189). Every later step of this reading, and most of the theme, builds on them.<span class="rm"><b>In this step you learn five things:</b><span><i>1</i>What job demands and job resources are, and why the list differs per job (rule 1)</span><span><i>2</i>The two processes they start: health impairment and motivation (rule 2)</span><span><i>3</i>That demands and resources also work together (rule 3)</span><span><i>4</i>Where personal resources fit in (rules 4 and 5)</span><span><i>5</i>How employees steer their own job, for better or worse (rules 6 and 7)</span></span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Job demands are the parts of a job that cost effort and energy; job resources are the parts that help you reach your goals, soften the demands and help you grow.</span>Every job, in JD-R, is made of these two ingredients. Together with the flexibility on the next card, this is rule 1. <b class="tm" data-g="demands">Job demands</b> require effort, so they come with a cost: physiological, meaning bodily (heart rate, oxygen use) or psychological (fatigue, anxiety). Examples the article gives later (p. 188): complex work problems, email overload. <b class="tm" data-g="resources">Job resources</b> are physical, psychological, social or organizational aspects of the job with <b>motivating potential</b>. They do three things: help you achieve work goals, reduce the impact of demands, and encourage learning and development. Examples from the article: constructive feedback, skill variety, support from colleagues. <i>Our example:</i> for a nurse, a full ward of patients is a demand; a colleague who takes over for ten minutes is a resource.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> to analyse any job with JD-R, you sort its features into two piles: what costs energy, and what helps.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: which features go in the piles? That depends on the job.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Because it gives no fixed list of job characteristics: every job or organization has its own demands and resources (rule 1).</span>Older job design theories did fix a list. <b>Job characteristics theory</b> (Hackman &amp; Oldham, 1976) and the <b>Michigan model</b> (French and colleagues, 1982) name the same characteristics for every job. JD-R does not. It assumes every organization and occupation is unique, so, in our example, a call-centre agent and a surgeon have different demands and resources. That is what the authors mean by <b class="tm" data-g="flex">flexible</b>. <i>Our example:</i> for a pilot, weather is a key demand; for a teacher, a noisy class is; neither is on a fixed list.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> if an exam option says "JD-R names the job characteristics that matter for every job", it is wrong. Common features such as workload or support appear in many jobs, but the theory does not prescribe them.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: what happens when demands keep coming?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Over a long time, high demands use up your physical, emotional and cognitive energy, so short-term fatigue can become chronic and serious health problems may arise: the health impairment process.</span>This is the first half of rule 2. The <b class="tm" data-g="hip">health impairment process</b> is the specific effect of demands on health, through using up energy. The article stresses time: it seems to work particularly over a longer period. The chain to remember: <b>demands → energy used up (strain) → health problems</b>. <i>Our example:</i> one evening of 200 emails makes you tired; months of it can leave you exhausted.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> demands are not bad by themselves on one day; the danger is in their lasting, high level.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the second half of rule 2, what resources do.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Job resources spark work engagement, and engaged employees perform better: the motivational process.</span>This is the second half of rule 2. In the <b class="tm" data-g="motproc">motivational process</b>, resources (the article's examples: decision latitude, time control) satisfy basic psychological needs, such as the need to belong and the need to do something meaningful in your own way. That feeds intrinsic motivation (wanting to do the work because it is satisfying in itself), which helps people keep going and perform. The chain: <b>resources → <span class="tm" data-g="engage">work engagement</span> → performance</b>. Work engagement is a state of high energy (vigor), enthusiasm about the content of your work (dedication) and focused attention on it (absorption). <i>Our example:</i> a designer who may choose how to tackle a project and gets good feedback throws herself into it.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> JD-R has two separate roads. Demands mainly drive the health road, resources mainly drive the motivation road. Exams often ask which road links resources to performance: the motivational one.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the two roads also meet.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ No: they also work together. Resources soften the effect of demands on strain (the buffer), and challenging demands make resources matter more for engagement (the boost).</span>This is rule 3: demands and resources have a <b>combined</b> impact on well-being (burnout and engagement). Two interactions:<span class="rm"><span><i>B</i><b class="tm" data-g="buffer">Buffer</b>: job resources weaken the impact of job demands on strain. Example from the article: support and feedback soften the effect of workload or conflicts on distress and burnout.</span><span><i>+</i><b class="tm" data-g="boost">Boost</b>: challenging demands strengthen the positive effect of job resources on engagement. Resources matter most when demands are high. The article's examples: work complexity and time pressure, met with feedback and colleague support.</span></span><i>Our example:</i> a hard deadline (challenging demand) plus a helpful team (resource) can make a week more engaging than a quiet week with the same team.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> the effect of a demand depends on the resources around it. Watch the pairing: buffer = resources and <b>strain</b>; boost = <b>challenging</b> demands and <b>engagement</b>. Step 5 works this out in graphs.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the person's own strengths.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Personal resources, such as optimism, self-efficacy and resilience, go hand in hand with job resources (rule 4) and change how demands affect well-being (rule 5).</span><b class="tm" data-g="persres">Personal resources</b> are positive beliefs about yourself: your sense that you can control and influence your environment successfully. <b>Rule 4</b>: they have a <b>reciprocal</b> relation with job resources: people with more optimism, self-efficacy and resilience tend to have more job resources, and the other way round. <b>Rule 5</b>: personal resources qualify, meaning change the size or direction of, the effect of demands. <b>The study:</b> Bakker and Sanz-Vergel (2013) followed nurses with a weekly diary. <i>What they found:</i> in weeks with emotionally demanding patient contacts (shown in a separate study to be challenging for nurses), nurses high in self-efficacy and optimism became <b>more</b> engaged, while nurses low in them became <b>less</b> engaged. <i>In plain words:</i> the same hard week energised confident nurses and lowered the others' engagement.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> a demand is not the same for everyone; the person's own resources help decide whether it drains or energises.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: people do not only react to their job; they also change it.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Yes: employees can proactively change their own demands and resources, called job crafting, and this raises fit, meaningfulness and engagement (rule 6).</span>Rule 6 makes the employee an actor. <b class="tm" data-g="crafting">Job crafting</b> (Wrzesniewski &amp; Dutton, 2001) is, in the definition of Tims and Bakker (2010), employees' personal initiative to change their job demands and resources so the job fits their abilities and preferences better. Forms they describe include role innovation, feedback seeking and task revision. Daily research adds: small daily adjustments, such as asking for support, starting a new project or finding a quiet place to focus, go with better daily well-being and performance. <i>Our example:</i> an accountant asks a senior colleague for feedback every Friday and swaps one dull task for a new client.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> in JD-R the job is not fixed: people can build their own resources and challenges. Theme 1's reading 5 is all about this.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the dark mirror of crafting.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Under persistently high demands and strain, people may fall into maladaptive self-regulation: avoidance coping and self-undermining, behavior that creates new obstacles for their own performance (rule 7).</span>Strain makes it harder to focus and leads to more mistakes; negative emotions such as anger and irritation narrow what people think of doing. So people may turn to destructive strategies: avoidance coping (dealing with stress by dodging the problem instead of tackling it) and <b class="tm" data-g="selfund">self-undermining</b>. The article's examples: poor communication, careless mistakes, starting conflicts. Each one creates new obstacles, in JD-R terms extra demands. <i>Our example:</i> an exhausted project leader snaps at a colleague, who then stops helping; now she has more work and less support.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> rules 6 and 7 are mirror images. Engagement and resources can lead to crafting, which adds resources; strain can lead to self-undermining, which adds demands. These are the seeds of the gain and loss spirals you meet later.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: check what you can now answer.</span></p>''',
'''<p class="recap"><span class="ans">⭐ JD-R in one line: two ingredients, two processes, two interactions, personal resources, and an employee who can make things better (crafting) or worse (self-undermining).</span><span class="rm"><span><i>1</i><b>What are demands and resources, and why is JD-R flexible?</b> Demands cost effort; resources help, soften and develop. No fixed list: each job has its own.</span><span><i>2</i><b>What are the two processes?</b> Health impairment: demands → exhaustion → health problems. Motivational: resources → engagement → performance.</span><span><i>3</i><b>How do they work together?</b> Buffer (resources soften demands' effect on strain) and boost (challenging demands make resources matter more for engagement).</span><span><i>4</i><b>Where do personal resources fit?</b> Reciprocal with job resources; they change how demands affect well-being (the nurse diary study).</span><span><i>5</i><b>How do employees steer their job?</b> Job crafting adds resources and challenges; self-undermining under strain adds obstacles.</span></span><b>The seven rules by number:</b><span class="rm"><span><i>1</i>Flexible: no fixed list of demands and resources</span><span><i>2</i>Two processes: health impairment and motivational</span><span><i>3</i>Demands and resources interact: buffer and boost</span><span><i>4</i>Personal and job resources are reciprocal</span><span><i>5</i>Personal resources change the effect of demands</span><span><i>6</i>Employees craft their job</span><span><i>7</i>Strain leads to maladaptive self-regulation: avoidance coping, self-undermining</span></span><b>Typical exam questions on this step:</b><span class="rm"><span><i>?</i>Which process links job resources to performance? (the motivational process, through engagement)</span><span><i>?</i>Which statement does NOT fit JD-R? (often: a fixed list of job characteristics)</span><span><i>?</i>A case: an employee asks for feedback or starts a new project. Crafting or self-undermining?</span><span><i>?</i>What did the nurse diary study show about personal resources?</span></span></p>''']
G='''<div class="qguide"><b>🎮 How to play</b><ol><li>The picture is the whole JD-R model: demands and resources on the left, strain and engagement in the middle, performance on the right.</li><li><b>Tap a numbered badge</b> (1 to 7). Each badge sits where that rule acts in the model.</li><li>Read the rule in plain words below the picture. Try to say first what the rule claims, then check.</li></ol></div>'''
def apply(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    i=s.index('id="w-r-faq"');a=s.index('<section class="step" data-t="Key concepts">',i);b=s.index('</section>',a)
    st=s[a:b]
    if 'data-v2' not in st[:80]: st=st.replace('<section class="step" ','<section class="step" data-v2="1" ',1)
    st=re.sub(r'<div class="ask">.*?</div>\n','',st,count=1)
    st=re.sub(r'<details class="exq">.*?</details>','',st,flags=re.S)
    if 'qguide' not in st:
        k=st.index('</svg>',st.index('id="faqModel"'))+6;st=st[:k]+G+st[k:]
    ti=st.index('<div class="teach"');tj=st.index('<div class="trapb">',ti)
    head=st[ti:tj];k=head.index('</div>',head.index('class="out"'))+6
    newhead='<div class="teach" data-lbl="'+LBL+'">'+head[head.index('<div class="out"'):k]+''.join(C)
    st=st[:ti]+newhead+st[tj:]
    s=s[:a]+st+s[b:]
    # ZERO for this step is replaced by the qstart card
    s,n=re.subn(r"\n'r-faq-3':'[^\n]*',?","\n",s,count=1);assert n==1
    open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
    assert len(LBL.split('|'))==len(C)-1
    return 'ok'
if __name__=='__main__': print(apply(sys.argv[1]))
